<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp7sec4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Orthogonality</title>


<p>
    The motivating geometric question of this lesson is: <em>What does it mean for vectors to be orthogonal?</em>
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-orthogonal">
    <title>Orthogonal</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Two vectors are <term>orthogonal</term> if the angle between them is a right angle.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
    Using our understanding of cosine and the Angle Equation \eqref{eq_inner_prod_cos}, it follows that 
</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-inner_prod_zero_orth">
    <title>Vectors are Orthogonal if their Inner Product is Zero</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in \R^m</m> are nonzero, then <m>\vect{v}</m> and <m>\vect{w}</m> are orthogonal if and only if <m>\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}} =0</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>

<definition xml:id="def-pairwise_orthogonal">
    <title>Pairwise Orthogonal Set</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>S \subset \R^m</m> is a finite collection of nonzero vectors, then <m>S</m> is <term>pairwise orthogonal</term> if for all <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in S</m>, <m>\vect{v}\ne \vect{w}</m>, then <m>\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}} = 0</m>.   
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<proposition xml:id="cqc-orthogonality">
    <title>Computational Quick Check for Orthogonality</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots \vect{v}_n\}\subset \R^m</m> and <m>A=[\vect{v}_1 \ | \ \cdots\ | \  \vect{v}_n]</m>, then
        </p>
        <p>
            <m>S</m> is pairwise orthogonal if and only if <m>A^TA</m> is diagonal.
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>

<proposition xml:id="prop-orth_implies_indep">
    <title>Pairwise Orthogonal Vectors are Independent</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>S\subset V</m> is a finite set of pairwise orthogonal (nonzero) vectors, then <m>S</m> is linearly independent.
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>

<p>
    Observe how this morally makes sense: <em>orthogonal vectors should be pointing in different directions</em>, and this is exactly what independence measures.
</p>

<proof>
    <title>Sketch of proof</title>
    <p>
        Let <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset \R^m</m> and <m>A=[\vect{v}_1 \ | \ \cdots\ | \  \vect{v}_n]</m>.
        Suppose <m>\vect{x}\in \R^n</m> is such that 
        <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m>. 
        Then <m>(A^TA)\vect{x}= A^T(A\vect{x})=A^T\vect{0}=\vect{0}</m>.
        Since <m>S</m> is orthogonal, Algorithm \ref{cqc-orthogonality} implies <m>A^TA</m> is diagonal (with nonzero diagonal entries).  
        Therefore <m>\vect{x}</m> must be <m>\vect{0}</m> and hence <m>S</m> is independent.
    </p>
</proof>

<definition>
    <title>Orthonormal Set</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            We call <m>S</m> <term>normal</term> if each vector in <m>S</m> is normal (i.e. if <m>\vect{v}\in S</m>, then <m>\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{v}}=1</m>).  
            If <m>S</m> is both pairwise orthogonal and normal, we call it <term>orthonormal</term>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<proposition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>S = \{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset \R^m</m> and <m>A  = [\vect{v}_1| \cdots | \vect{v}_n]</m>.   
            Then <m>S</m> is orthonormal if and only if <m>A^TA = I_{n\times n}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>

<definition>
    <title>Orthogonal Matrix</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>Q</m> is <term>orthogonal</term> if <m>Q^T Q = I_{n\times n}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
    It follows that if <m>S</m> is orthonormal, <em>it is an orthonormal basis for its span</em>.
</p>

<p>
    <em>Why do we care?  </em>
    Bases are necessary to perform computations.  
    Basis give us coordinates!  
    Orthonormal basis give us nice crisp square coordinate grids.  Certain computations become much easier with ``good'' orthogonal coordinates.
</p>

<p>
    So the question becomes, given a basis <m>S</m> for a subspace <m>W\subset \R^n</m>, <em>how do you find an orthonormal basis for <m>W</m></em>?
</p>

<p>
    One strategy for this is the Gram--Schmidt Orthogonalization Algorithm, which we will introduce in a few sections.
</p>


</section>